==English==
[[File:Neptune and Tritron - GPN-2000-000442.jpg|right|thumb|Neptune and Triton; the moon is between 5 and 6 o'clock with respect to Neptune.]]

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|a clock||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
Shortened form of "''of the [[clock]]''", from {{inh|en|enm|of the [[clokke]]}} {{century|late 14}}.<ref>[https://www.etymonline.com/word/o%27clock "o'clock"] &ndash; Etymonline]</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/əˈklɒk/|/əʊˈklɒk/|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/əˈklɑk/|/oʊˈklɑk/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-o'clock.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɒk|s=2}}

===Adverb===
{{en-adv|-|head=[[o']][[clock]]}}

# {{lb|en|modifying a numeral, one to twelve}} In conjunction with a numeral, indicates the [[time]] within a twelve-hour period ([[midnight]] to [[noon]] or noon to midnight), specifically the time when the hour hand of a clock points precisely to the symbol or marking [[corresponding]] to the [[designated]] numeral.
#: {{ux|en|We are expected to be there at six '''o'clock''' in the morning!}}
#: {{ux|en|It is two '''o'clock'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|(''rare'', ''nonstandard'') What '''o'clock''' is it?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1892|year_published='''2011'''|edition=HTML|author=Sir {{w|Arthur Conan Doyle}}|title=w:The Adventures of Sherlock Holmes|url=http://www.gutenberg.org/files/1661/1661-h/1661-h.htm|publisher=w:The Gutenberg Project|passage=I say, Watson, what '''o'clock''' is it?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1935|author=[https://www.goodreads.com/author/show/288354.George_Goodchild George Goodchild]
|title=Death on the Centre Court|chapter=5
|passage=By one '''o'clock''' the place was choc-a-bloc. […] The restaurant was packed, and the promenade between the two main courts and the subsidiary courts was thronged with healthy-looking youngish people, drawn to the Mecca of tennis from all parts of the country.}}
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=1955
|author=[[w:Harry S. Truman|Harry S Truman]]
|chapter=1
|title=Memoirs of Harry S. Truman: Year of Decisions
|url=https://archive.org/details/yearofdecisionsv030151mbp/
|year_published=1965
|volume=I
|publisher=w:Signet Books
|OCLC=633884574
|OL=10270721M
|page=14
|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/yearofdecisionsv030151mbp/page/14/
|text=Shortly before five '''o'clock''' in the afternoon of Thursday, April 12, 1945, after the Senate adjourned, I went to the office of House Speaker Sam Rayburn. I went there to get an agreement between the Speaker and the Vice-President on certain legislation and to discuss the domestic and world situation generally. As I entered, the Speaker told me that Steve Early, the President's press secretary, had just telephoned, requesting me to call the White House.}}
# {{lb|en|jocular}} In conjunction with a term representing an action or event that occurs daily, indicates the time that said action or event occurs, first occurs.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1880|author=Henrietta A. Duff|title=Honor Carmichael|pageurl=http://books.google.com/books?id=ivMBAAAAQAAJ&q=%22dinner+o%27clock%22&dq=%22dinner+o%27clock%22&hl=en&ei=R_cqTsPtNorsrQfb74WyDQ&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=7&ved=0CEkQ6AEwBg|page=251
|passage=That same evening at tea-time — (I am sorry to have to introduce you to another eating-scene, but the hours in English households are usually marked by repasts. It is a daily calendar of feasts — breakfast '''o’clock''', dinner '''o’clock''', &c.,{{...}}).}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1904|author=w:George Augustus Sala; Edmund Hodgson Yates|title=[[w:Temple Bar (magazine)|Temple Bar]]|volume=129|pageurl=http://books.google.com/books?id=OU83AAAAMAAJ&q=%22supper+o%27clock%22&dq=%22supper+o%27clock%22&hl=en&ei=hvEqTu3eOcHsrAf4w_mxDQ&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=5&ved=0CDoQ6AEwBA|page=144
|passage=“My sister requires your attendance at supper '''o’clock''' this evening — no excuse accepted.”}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1998|author=Carolyn Greene|title=Heavenly Husband|pageurl=http://books.google.com/books?id=ovdrZZmRa_wC&q=%22lunch%7Cluncheon+o%27clock%22&dq=%22lunch%7Cluncheon+o%27clock%22&hl=en&ei=0PUqTuaZIc3JrAfZ3bSyDQ&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=6&ved=0CD0Q6AEwBQ|page=129
|passage=“It's lunch '''o’clock'''. Wanna go out to eat?”}}
# {{lb|en|jocular|slang}} Used to indicate that it is time to do a specific action, or time for a specific action to occur.
#: {{usex|en|We're here at Waffle House, and it's waffles '''o'clock'''!|t=We're here at Waffle House, and it's '''time to''' eat waffles.}}
# {{lb|en|with a numeral, one to twelve}} In conjunction with a numeral, indicates the direction, relative to the speaker or a vehicle, especially an aircraft, corresponding to the direction the hour hand is pointing at the time corresponding to the numeral, with twelve representing directly [[ahead]] on a horizontal plane, or directly up on a vertical plane, and three being to the [[right]] on either.
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Birding around the year: when to find birds in North America|author=Aileen R. Lotz|year=1989|passage=It's at nine '''o'clock''' about two feet in from the edge of the branch. You need to know your "''o'clocks''" (nondigital) to spot birds.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The Wellness book|page=86|author=Herbert Benson|coauthors=Eileen M. Stuart; Staff of the Mind/Body Medical Institute of New England Deaconess Hospital and Harvard Medical School|year=1992|passage=Without moving your head, move your gaze upward to look at twelve '''o’clock'''. Now look down at six '''o’clock'''. Repeat three times, then blink several times, and rest with your eyes closed.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Laboratory outlines in biology--VI|page=22|author=Peter Abramoff|coauthors=Robert G. Thomson|year=1994|passage=Thus, you could tell them to "look at three '''o'clock'''," or "look just off-center toward nine '''o'clock'''," and so forth. Alternatively, in some microscopes a thin black line appears to cut across the field of view}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Bird watching for dummies|author=Bill Thompson|year=1997|passage=Three '''o'clock''' and nine '''o'clock''' would be at the outer right and outer left sides of the tree, respectively, and so on. The clock technique is a very helpful way for one bird watcher to direct others to a hard-to-spot perched bird.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=November 29 1943|title=Target: Germany|journal=[[w:Life (magazine)|Life]]|pageurl=http://books.google.com/books?id=DVcEAAAAMBAJ&pg=PA80&dq=%22flak%22+-intitle:flak&hl=en&ei=4jkpTujsJYjMmAWg8p2ZDA&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=2&ved=0CCwQ6AEwATgU#v=onepage&q=%22flak%22%20-intitle%3Aflak&f=false|page=80
|passage=''At 1057 we were just over the islands and at 1100 the tail gunner reported flak at six '''o’clock''', below.''}}

